# Half-Life HD shotgun (SPAS-12). Measured by hand off valve_hd's model,
# because vrcardgen returns NOTHING for this rig - see the next paragraph.
#
# ONE CARD, THREE GAME DIRECTORIES. valve_hd, gearbox_hd and bshift_hd ship
# three v_shotgun.mdl files of three different SIZES - 167880, 205856 and
# 97992 bytes - and they are NOT byte-identical. What they share is the RIG:
# 52 bones, 10 sequences, hash 0xf086fed6, and the same 3.4298-unit fore-end
# travel in all three. Only the arm and hand mesh differs (the weapon's share
# of the vertices is 41.3 / 41.1 / 42.2% of 1021 / 1027 / 999), and the
# fingerprint does not look at vertices. So this one card binds to all three.
#
# VRCARDGEN FINDS NO JOINTS ON THIS MODEL, AND IT IS WRONG. It reports "0
# drivable joints" and drafts a card that says the weapon has nothing
# drivable. The cause is find_body (vrcardgen.py:474): it starts from the
# first attachment and takes whichever of the muzzle's ancestors-and-their-
# children owns the most mesh. On this rig the one attachment is on bone 35
# `Bip01 R Hand` - the HD animators hung the muzzle flash off the hand, not
# off the gun - so the search never sees the weapon at all and settles on
# `R_Arm_bone`, 6.2%. Everything that is actually the shotgun is a SIBLING of
# that chain, exactly like the v_egon case the function's own comment
# describes. Measured here by dumping every bone instead.
#
# THE WEAPON IS FOUR BONES, and they are the only root-parented subtree in
# the model that is not Bip01:
#
#   15  Reciever   root-parented, 422 verts (41.3%)   the body
#   16  Stock      child of Reciever, 195 verts       never animated
#   17  Stock2     child of Stock,     27 verts       never animated
#   18  Charger    child of Reciever,  47 verts (4.6%) THE FORE-END
#
# `Charger` is the pump, and it is the only thing on the gun that moves
# relative to the gun. Its rest transform relative to `Reciever` is the
# identity - offset (0,0,0), rotation (0,0,0), axes measured parallel to the
# body's to four decimals - so every number below is simultaneously in the
# bone's space and in the weapon's.
#
# Its vertices span Y -18.32..-6.66 and Z -4.81..+0.70 in the body's frame,
# wrapping under and around the magazine tube (the body's own geometry sits at
# Z ~ -3 across Y -28..-16). Forward is -Y: the muzzle attachment resolves to
# (1.75 -25.59 3.72) in the body's frame. So the fore-end is below the bore,
# ahead of the receiver, and it travels +Y, which is REARWARD. A pump pulled
# back. There is nothing else it could be.
#
# `shell` (bone 14) is NOT a joint. It is parented to `Bip01 L Finger02`,
# eleven bones into the left arm, and it is the shell the hand carries - an
# arriving object the feed path owns, not a part of the weapon.

weapon v_shotgun
match  bones 52  seqs 10  hash 0xf086fed6

# The bone every position in this card is measured in - the controls, the
# grab points, the chamber, the ejection port. Nothing recorded it until
# the engine had to place a control in the world to ask whether a thumb is
# near it; the simulator never needed one, because it never works in world
# space.
#
# IT SAID `R_Arm_bone` AND THAT WAS WRONG - an arm, 63 vertices, offset 8.55
# units from its parent and rotated by (1.26, -1.55, -1.28). Every position in
# this card is measured in `Reciever`, the 422-vertex bone that is the gun, and
# the grab point below sits 9.9 units outside the arm's bounds. Caught by
# tools/vrcard/card_audit.py, which checks a card's declared points against
# the bone it says they are in.
#
# WHY THE GENERATOR GOT IT WRONG, because it will do it again: find_body starts
# from the first attachment - "on a view model that is the muzzle, which is on
# the weapon by definition" - and walks UP the parent chain taking whichever
# ancestor owns the most mesh. On this model the attachment is on `Bip01 R
# Hand` and `Reciever` has NO PARENT AT ALL: the gun is a separate root, not
# parented into the arm rig. So walking up from the hand can never reach it,
# and the best ancestor is an arm.
#
# The grab point needs no change, and that is the one lucky part: `Charger` is
# parented to `Reciever` with a bind position of exactly 0,0,0 and no rotation,
# so its own frame and the receiver's are the same frame. The number was always
# right; only the name of the frame was wrong.
body   "Reciever"

# 8 is SHOTGUN_MAX_CLIP, dlls/weapons.h:128.
#
# 300 rpm IS DELIBERATELY LOOSE, and the looseness is measured. The pump is
# the limiter on this weapon, and the only thing an rpm can add is a second,
# invisible timer that refuses a shot the player has earned. The floor the
# model itself demonstrates: `pump` runs at 30 fps and the fore-end goes back
# over frames 4-8 and forward over 8-11 - seven frames, 233 ms for a complete
# cycle. 300 rpm is a 200 ms gate, under that floor, so a hand that has
# actually worked the action is never told to wait. (hl.dll's own 0.75 s
# between shots, shotgun.cpp:178, is 80 rpm; that number paces an animation
# nobody is playing any more.)
type   tube_pump  capacity 8  rpm 300

# NO `feed` LINE, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT ANSWER rather than an omission. The
# tube_pump preset sets VRFEED_FL_TUBE and nothing else (vr_gun.cpp:109-117):
# no self_loading, because a pump gun is worked by hand, and no slide_lock,
# because a pump has nothing to lock it open. Both of the flags the other
# firearm cards have to clear are already clear here. Restating them would be
# noise.

# JOINT 0: the fore-end, and the preset's action joint - tube_pump declares
# exactly one joint and sets feed.action_joint = 0, so `Charger` has to be 0.
#
# Travel 3.43 is measured, not quoted: posed through all ten sequences with
# the bone's position taken relative to its own rest pose, `Charger` moves
# 3.4298 units along the body's +Y and rotates by EXACTLY nothing - all three
# rotation channels are flat to the last bit in every frame of every
# sequence. A clean straight stroke. It moves in three sequences and only
# three: `pump`, `shoot` and `shoot_big`, because Half-Life pumps the gun as
# part of firing. Once a hand owns the joint the animation is irrelevant.
#
# (The SD rig's fore-end is also called `Charger` and also travels ~3.42,
# which is why binding by name would have been a coin flip and binding by
# fingerprint is not. The two are different models with different bone counts
# and different hashes.)
#
# The grab point is the centroid of the fore-end's 47 vertices, which is in
# the body's frame for the reason given above. The radius is measured the
# same way the MP5's is: the furthest of those vertices is 3.23 units from
# the centroid in the plane ACROSS the travel axis, so 3.2 is the hand's
# reach, not a guess. (In 3D the furthest is 6.42, but that is half the
# fore-end's length, not its thickness.)
joint 0  bone "Charger"  slide  travel 3.43  rest 0
    grab  point 0.82 -12.59 -2.49  radius 3.2

# THE FEED AND EJECTION POINTS KEEP THE PRESET'S 0.40 AND 0.85, and that is
# honest rather than lazy. Nothing in this model shows a round moving through
# the action: there is no case bone, no round bone on the gun, and the three
# sequences the fore-end moves in move the fore-end and the body and nothing
# else. Where in the stroke a shell actually feeds or leaves has NOT been
# measured, and a fabricated number would be worse than a default that is
# openly a default.
#
# THE LOADING GATE HAS NO FIELD, SO HERE IS THE MEASUREMENT instead, for
# whoever adds one. `reload` (seq 3, 16 fps) carries `shell` from the pouch
# to the gate over frames 1-4, in the body's frame:
#
#   f1  base (  0.02  -2.36  -9.38)
#   f2  base ( -0.09  -3.53  -5.80)
#   f3  base (  0.11  -4.59  -3.03)
#   f4  base (  0.14  -5.70  -2.28)   tip ( -0.00  -7.20  -1.18)
#
# So the shell arrives from below and behind and is pushed tip-first up and
# forward along (-0.08 -0.81 +0.59), ending under the receiver at Y ~ -5.7.
# The receiver's underside across Y -8..-4 bottoms out at Z -0.23, so the
# gate mouth is that spot on the belly of the gun - which is where a SPAS-12
# keeps it. Note that at full travel the fore-end's rear edge reaches Y -3.23
# and covers this: on the real gun you load with the action forward.
#
# NO `chamber at`. Part D's press check wants the chambered shell drawn at a
# declared position, and this model has no round in the chamber to measure -
# `shell` is only ever in the left hand. Picking the breech by eye off a
# preview is a different job from measuring, and this card does not pretend
# to have done it.
#
# NO `eject at`. hl.dll throws shotgun brass from a view-space fudge
# (ev_hldm.cpp:561 and :614, forward 32 / right -12 / up 6) which puts the
# hull out of the LEFT side of the gun and is not a measurement of this model
# at all. There is no ejection-port bone to measure instead.
#
# NO `fire` LINE. The preset's VRTrigger_DefaultCfg already gives it semi and
# only semi, which is what a pump gun has. PART J'S DOUBLE-BARREL SECONDARY -
# "pulling through a second stop fires both" - IS NOT ON THIS CARD, because
# the format cannot say it: break_at and reset_at are trigger config the card
# grammar does not expose, and a two-stage trigger needs a second break point
# and a two-round shot. `shoot_big` (seq 2) exists and pumps the same single
# fore-end, so the content is there waiting; the card format is what is
# missing.
#
# NO `strike`. Every firearm inherits the weak pistol-whip default from
# VRStrike_DefaultCfg, as the other firearm cards do. A shotgun butt-stroke
# deserves better than a pistol whip and `Stock`/`Stock2` are right there
# with 222 vertices between them - but nothing about that butt has been
# measured, and the default is at least not a wrong measurement.
